History is replete with examples of group decisions overriding realistic and sensible courses of action. Two well-known psychological phenomena at the root of such group behavior are known as the risky shift effect and the "groupthink" hypothesis. Research into the former reveals that decisions taken by a group after discussion display more experimentation, are less conservative, and are more risky than those made by individuals acting alone prior to any discussion. It appears, then, that members of a group increase their tendency to engage in reckless behavior as a result of working with other members. For example, a group of managers or, on the other hand, a committee of union members is more likely to adopt a radical and perhaps confrontational approach to negotiations than any of the individuals acting separately.

Such risky behavior often goes hand in hand with the tendency of group members to become susceptible to "groupthink," a notion developed by Irving Janis whereby each individual tries to conform to what they perceive is the disposition of the group as a whole. They may be overtaken by an illusion of group invulnerability and may take on an attitude of excessive optimism.  Furthermore, members may start to believe in their group's inherent righteousness.  The group conformity is further cemented by a process of self-censorship, which prevents individuals from giving voice to misgivings. These biases can become so dominant that members fail to make realistic evaluations of the outcomes of their decisions. }

A much-cited historical illustration of groupthink is the decision by the Kennedy Administration to order the disastrous Bay of Pigs invasion of Cuba in 1961. Individual advisors of the president who initially questioned the wisdom of the invasion modified their views after group discussion and concurred with the risky venture. Though such political blunders furnish the most salient examples of these phenomena, the dangers created by the inclinations of group members to make irrational decisions are evident in all human endeavors.
